Liquid petroleum, or crude oil, contains a variety of hydrocarbons. When crude oil is extracted from the earth and then processed in oil refineries, a step called "fractional distillation" is used to separate the different types of hydrocarbons according to their boiling points. This process involves heating the crude oil, which causes the different components to evaporate. The vaporized compounds are then cooled and condensed, with the lighter hydrocarbons (which have lower boiling points) belonging to a group called "gasoline," settling in one container. The heavier hydrocarbons will boil at higher temperatures and remain as more viscous products such as diesel and heating oil.

Some of the specific processes used in a modern refinery to turn crude oil into gasoline involve:

1. Cracking: Breaking down long chain hydrocarbons into shorter chain ones;

2. Aromatics separation: Separating aromatics (cyclic hydrocarbons) from aliphatic (open-chain) hydrocarbons, as these have different chemical and physical properties;

3. Isomerisation: Converting straight-chain aliphatics into isomers (having a different molecular structure but the same chemical formula) with improved octane ratings and burning characteristics.

Essentially, refining of crude oil produces several different products, including gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, as well as waxes, asphalt, crude oil derivatives (such as plastics, fertilizers, detergents, and pesticides), and other products depending on the structure and composition of local petroleum deposits.

