TypeScript allows us to specify the "discriminated union type" which indicates that a value could belong to multiple types, but the type it will ultimately have is identified by a specific property (called the discriminator). This property can be used to narrow down the context of the value to a specific type.

Here is an example illustrating how to tell TypeScript "this value will only have one of these types":

```typescript
type Animal = { type: "animal", age: number, belongingType?: string } | { type: "animal", age: number, belongingType: "pet" };

function createAnimal(species: "pet" | "pet" | "animal", age?: number) {
  const animal: Animal = { type: "animal", age };

  // The "belongingType" property can be used to narrow down the context of the value to a specific type (in this case, it is "pet")
  if (species === "pet") {
    animal.belongingType = "pet";
  }

  return animal;
}

const cat = createAnimal("pet", 5);
const dog = createAnimal("pet", 3);
const bear = createAnimal("animal", 20);
console.log(cat);  // { type: "animal", age: 5, belongingType: "pet" }
console.log(dog);  // { type: "animal", age: 3, belongingType: "pet" }
console.log(bear);  // { type: "animal", age: 20 }
```

In this example, `Animal` is a discriminated union type, which means it can either be an animal that belongs to the `pet` type or an animal that belongs to the `animal` type. The `belongingType` property is used to narrow down the context of the value to a specific type.

In the example, `createAnimal` defines the `Animal` object and uses the `if` statement to set the value of `belongingType` based on the `species` parameter, thereby ensuring that the value is of a single type that can be reliably used and expected by the type system.

